---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArrowIcon from '../components/ArrowIcon.astro';
import '../styles/our-story.css';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Our Story"
  navCurrent="story"
  description="Why I built Alexandra's — a founder's note on three countries, a village childhood, and the encouragement to be brave."
>

  {/* ── Header ──────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="page-header-inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">Our Story</span>
      <h1 class="display">Why I <em>built</em> this.</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  {/* ── Story body ──────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <article class="section story">

    <p class="story-lead">
      I was a child who could sing. My teachers knew it; I knew it. But when the
      moment came to stand on a stage and let anyone hear me, I couldn't. No one
      had taught me it was safe to be seen — to be imperfect in front of other
      people — so I stayed in my seat. I never performed. The voice I had simply
      faded. I'm an adult now, and I'm still afraid of stages. That small,
      private regret is one of the reasons this centre exists.
    </p>

    <figure class="story-figure">
      <div class="story-figure-img" data-label="photo: a portrait of our founder — ideally with Alexandra"></div>
    </figure>

    <p>
      I grew up inside the Chinese education system, in Jiangsu, where the path
      to a future runs across what we call the <em>du mu qiao</em> — the
      single-plank bridge that thousands cross and few get over. I made it; I
      finished in the top 5%. I don't resent the discipline that took. It gave me
      foundations, and a belief I still hold: that ability is <em>built</em>
      through effort, not handed out at birth.
    </p>

    <p>
      Underneath the exams sits something older and gentler — the Confucian idea
      that education isn't only about becoming clever, but about becoming a good
      person: respect for learning, respect for your elders, the patience to
      practise. I want that inheritance for these children. But I've felt its
      other edge, too. The same culture that teaches a child to be modest and to
      defer can also teach them not to put themselves forward — not to sing on
      the stage. I want the gift without the cost.
    </p>

    <p>
      And when I ask what learning actually stayed with me, it isn't the exams at
      all. It's my grandparents' village. The river where we caught crabs and
      fish. Stones and rubber bands for toys. Drawing on the floor. Turning a
      corner of the yard into a house, a police station, a whole world. We
      learned to read and write from the older children long before any teacher
      made it formal. That was real learning too — maybe the realest.
    </p>

    <blockquote class="story-quote">
      <p>Play is not the opposite of learning.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      My husband grew up in Singapore, where discipline sits at the centre of
      childhood — and where children grow up in two languages at once, English
      alongside their mother tongue, from the very beginning. Between us we
      carried two versions of structure and a real respect for what it gives a
      child: focus, self-control, the habits that make everything else possible.
      I also came to believe that holding two languages early isn't only good for
      a young mind — it's how a child keeps a door open to where they come from.
      Keep the mother tongue, and you keep the grandparents, the village, the
      whole world behind the words.
    </p>

    <p>
      So when it was our turn — our daughter, Alexandra — we couldn't stop asking
      the question. We read, we argued, we tried childcare and kindergarten in
      China, in Singapore, and here in Australia. And in Australia I found the
      thing my own childhood had been missing a name for: children trusted to
      follow their own curiosity, playing outdoors in real weather, allowed to be
      confident and to speak — to wonder out loud, to take up space.
    </p>

    <p>
      I don't want to choose between these worlds. I want the best of all three,
      for Alexandra and for every child here: the foundations, the character, and
      the belief in effort I inherited from China; the discipline and the two
      languages my husband brought from Singapore; the curiosity, the nature, and
      the confidence I found in Australia. Not one at the cost of another — each
      in its right place. Structure to give a child security. Freedom to let them
      explore. And, above all, the thing I was never given: the encouragement to
      be brave. To try. To be wrong out loud. To take the stage.
    </p>

    <p>
      There's one more question beneath all of it, one I've carried since I
      arrived here: where do we belong? My generation often feels we belong here
      — or nowhere; never quite of the place we left, never quite of the place we
      chose. I won't hand that uncertainty to my daughter. I want Alexandra to
      belong without shrinking any part of herself — wholly at home in Australia,
      wholly proud of where she comes from, never made to choose. That, to me, is
      what <em>becoming</em> should mean for our children: not becoming less of
      one world to fit another, but becoming big enough to hold both.
    </p>

    <p>
      That's the centre I went three countries to learn how to build. It carries
      my daughter's name.
    </p>

    <p class="story-byline">— [Founder's name], founder</p>

  </article>

  {/* ── Closing CTA ─────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="final-cta">
    <h2 class="display">I'd love to show it to <em>you</em>.</h2>
    <p>
      Tours run on weekday mornings and take about forty minutes — enough to
      walk through, meet a few educators, and watch the day unfold.
    </p>
    <a href="/book-a-tour" class="btn-primary">
      Book a Tour
      <ArrowIcon />
    </a>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
